---
title: House Black Daal
time: 4-5 hours
servings: 8
source: Dishoom cookbook
tags:
  - indian
  - daal
  - dishoom
---

-- ============================================================
-- COOK THE DAAL
-- ============================================================

Put the @whole black urad daal{300%g} into a #large bowl{}, cover with water and whisk for ~{10%seconds}. Let the daal settle, then pour out the water. Repeat 3 or 4 times, until the water is clear.

Tip the daal into a #large saucepan{} and pour in at least @cold water{4%L}. Bring to the boil and cook steadily for ~{2-3%hours}. Skim off any impurities that rise to the surface, and add more boiling water as required to keep the grains well covered. The grains need to become completely soft, with the skins coming away from the white grain. When pressed, the white part should be creamy, rather than crumbly. When cooked, turn off the heat and let the pan sit for ~{15%minutes}.

-- ============================================================
-- AROMATIC PASTE
-- ============================================================

In a #bowl{}, mix the @garlic paste{12%g}, @ginger paste{10%g}, @tomato purée{70%g}, @fine sea salt{8%g}, @deggi mirch chilli powder{2/3%tsp} and @garam masala{1/3%tsp} to a paste.

-- ============================================================
-- SIMMER UNTIL CREAMY
-- ============================================================

Carefully pour off the daal cooking water, then pour on enough freshly boiled water to cover the daal by 3–4cm. Bring to the boil over a medium-high heat and add the aromatic paste and @unsalted butter{90%g}. Cook rapidly for ~{30%minutes}, stirring regularly to prevent the mixture from sticking.

Lower the heat and simmer for ~{1-1.5%hours}, stirring very regularly to prevent it from sticking and adding a little boiling water if the liquid level gets near the level of the grains. Eventually it will become thick and creamy. The creaminess must come from the grains disintegrating into the liquid, not from the water evaporating and leaving only the grains behind.

Add the @double cream{90%ml} and cook for a further ~{15%minutes}. Serve with @chapatis{} or other Indian breads.

-- Reheating leftovers: you may need more liquid; use cream, or cream and water, rather than water alone.
